/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/google-cdl.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("google-cdl", [["cdl1",0,1,"An online ticket seller gets ten times its normal traffic for a few hours when a big concert goes on sale, then traffic drops back. Which cloud characteristic helps most?",["Fixed capacity sized for the average day, which keeps monthly spending predictable","Elasticity: capacity is added for the spike and released afterward","Buying enough servers for the peak and leaving them idle the rest of the time","A longer contract with a colocation provider for extra rack space"],1,"Elasticity lets capacity grow and shrink automatically with demand, so the seller pays for the spike only while it lasts. Sizing for the average would fail at peak, and buying for the peak leaves expensive hardware idle.","Section 1.1",["Capacity sized for the average day would be overwhelmed by a tenfold spike, so customers would see errors during the sale.",null,"Buying for the peak works technically, but the hardware sits idle most of the year, which is the waste elasticity avoids.","More colocation rack space is still hardware you buy and manage; it cannot appear for a few hours and then disappear."],null],["cdl2",0,1,"A finance director wants to stop making large up-front hardware purchases every three years and pay each month for the IT resources actually used. What change is this?",["Shifting spending from operating expenditure to capital expenditure","Turning variable IT costs into fixed long-term costs","Increasing the depreciation schedule on owned servers","Shifting spending from capital expenditure to operating expenditure"],3,"Up-front purchases of depreciating assets are CapEx; paying for use as you go is OpEx. The cloud moves most IT spending from CapEx to OpEx and turns fixed costs into variable ones, not the other way round.","Section 1.2",["This is the reverse: buying hardware up front is capital expenditure, and paying monthly for use is operating expenditure.","Cloud pay-as-you-go does the opposite, turning fixed hardware costs into variable costs that follow usage.","Depreciation applies to owned assets; the director wants to stop buying them, so depreciation would shrink, not grow.",null],null],["cdl3",0,1,"When comparing an on-premises data center with Google Cloud, a company counts only server purchase prices. Which costs are they most likely leaving out of the total cost of ownership?",["Power, cooling, floor space, maintenance contracts and staff time","The monthly price of the virtual machines they plan to run in the cloud after the move","The cost of the application licenses they already own","The number of users who will access the application"],0,"Total cost of ownership includes all direct and indirect costs over time: facilities, power and cooling, hardware refresh, maintenance and the people who run it. Hardware price alone understates on-premises cost.","Section 1.2",[null,"The cloud price is the other side of the comparison and is already counted; the question is about on-premises costs that were left out.","Licenses the company already owns are a cost in both scenarios, not the hidden facilities and staff costs of running a data center.","The number of users affects capacity planning but is not itself a cost item in a total cost of ownership calculation."],null],["cdl4",0,1,"A bank keeps its core ledger in its own data center for regulatory reasons but runs its customer analytics and mobile back end in Google Cloud, connected over a private link. Which deployment model is this?",["Multicloud","Public cloud only","Private cloud only","Hybrid cloud"],3,"Hybrid cloud combines on-premises or private infrastructure with a public cloud. Multicloud means using more than one public cloud provider, which this scenario does not describe.","Section 1.2",["Multicloud means using two or more public cloud providers; here there is one public cloud plus the bank's own data center.","The ledger stays in the bank's own data center, so not everything runs in the public cloud.","Part of the workload runs in Google Cloud, a public cloud, so this is not a private-cloud-only setup.",null],null],["cdl5",0,1,"A company uses Google Cloud for analytics and another public cloud provider for its e-commerce site, and has no data center of its own. How is this best described?",["Hybrid cloud","Edge computing","Private cloud","Multicloud"],3,"Using two or more public cloud providers is multicloud. Hybrid would require on-premises or private cloud infrastructure in the mix, and the company has none.","Section 1.2",["Hybrid cloud needs on-premises or private cloud infrastructure, and this company has no data center of its own.","Edge computing means processing data near where it is created, such as in stores or devices, not using two cloud providers.","A private cloud is infrastructure dedicated to one organization; both providers here are public clouds.",null],null],["cdl6",0,1,"A startup wants to run its code without managing operating systems, patches or servers, and only upload the application. Which service model fits best?",["Infrastructure as a service (IaaS)","On-premises virtualization","Platform as a service (PaaS)","Colocation hosting"],2,"PaaS provides the runtime and platform so the customer brings only code and data. IaaS would leave the operating system and patching to the startup.","Section 1.3",["With IaaS the startup would still install, patch and manage the operating system on its VMs.","On-premises virtualization means running and patching your own servers and hypervisors, the opposite of what the startup wants.",null,"Colocation rents space for hardware you own and manage yourself, so servers and patching stay with the startup."],null],["cdl7",0,1,"Which statement about the shared responsibility model is correct?",["Google is responsible for customer data once it is stored in Google Cloud","Customers are always responsible for their data and who can access it","The customer's share of responsibility is the same in IaaS and SaaS","Moving to SaaS makes the customer responsible for the physical data center"],1,"In every service model the customer keeps responsibility for its data, identities and access. The provider's share grows from IaaS to SaaS, so the split is not the same across models.","Section 1.3",["Google secures the infrastructure, but the customer stays responsible for its data and who can access it.",null,"The customer's share is largest in IaaS and smallest in SaaS; it changes with the service model.","Physical data centers are always the provider's responsibility; SaaS moves more work to the provider, not less."],null],["cdl8",0,1,"An organization moves an application from Compute Engine VMs to a fully managed SaaS product. How does its security responsibility change?",["It must now also secure the physical servers","It no longer needs to control who has access to the data","It stays exactly the same as on VMs, because the data and the users have not changed at all","It shrinks, because the provider now manages the platform and the application"],3,"With SaaS the provider manages the infrastructure, platform and application, so the customer's share shrinks, but the customer still owns its data and access management.","Section 1.3",["Physical servers are the provider's job in every cloud model, and even more so with SaaS.","Access to data remains the customer's responsibility in every model, including SaaS.","The split does change: with SaaS the provider takes over the OS, platform and application that the customer managed on VMs.",null],null],["cdl9",0,1,"An application must keep running if one data center building in a region loses power. What is the simplest Google Cloud design choice?",["Run instances in more than one zone of the region","Run all instances in a single zone with larger machines","Store backups on the same VM's disk","Buy a dedicated interconnect to the region"],0,"Zones are isolated deployment areas within a region, so spreading instances across zones survives a single zone failure. Larger machines or local backups in one zone still share its failure.","Section 1.2",[null,"All instances in a single zone share that zone's failure; bigger machines do not help when the building loses power.","Backups on the same VM's disk are lost with the VM and do nothing to keep the application running.","Interconnect improves connectivity from on-premises; it does not protect against a zone failure inside the region."],null],["cdl10",0,1,"Users in Australia complain that a web app hosted only in a US region is slow to respond, even though the servers show low CPU use. What is the most likely cause?",["Not enough bandwidth on the users' home connections, which limits how much data can arrive at once","The app uses too many storage classes","Network latency from the distance between users and the region","The project has too many IAM roles"],2,"Latency is the time for data to travel, and it grows with distance. Serving content from a closer region or edge locations (for example with a CDN) reduces it. Low CPU shows the servers are not the bottleneck.","Section 1.2",["Limited bandwidth slows large downloads, but interactive delay from users on another continent is mainly caused by distance (latency).","Storage classes affect storage price and retrieval cost, not the round-trip time between Australia and the US.",null,"IAM roles control permissions and have no effect on network response time."],null],["cdl11",0,1,"What do Google's edge points of presence mainly do for Google Cloud customers?",["Store customers' primary databases in every city so data never leaves the user's country","Bring traffic onto Google's private network close to users, reducing latency","Replace the need for regions and zones","Provide on-premises hardware installed at customer sites"],1,"Points of presence are where Google's network connects to other networks near users, so traffic rides Google's private backbone for most of its trip and content can be cached at the edge. Regions and zones still host the workloads.","Section 1.2",["Edge locations do not host customers' primary databases; workloads and data live in the regions the customer chooses.",null,"PoPs complement regions and zones; you still run workloads in regions.","PoPs are Google network locations, not hardware installed at customer sites."],null],["cdl12",0,1,"A CIO is worried about being locked in to one provider. Which Google Cloud approach most directly addresses this?",["Signing a multi-year committed use contract so that prices stay fixed for longer periods","Using a single region for all workloads","Storing data only in the Archive storage class","Building on open source and open standards such as Kubernetes"],3,"Open source and open standards (Kubernetes, Apache Beam, TensorFlow, PostgreSQL) let workloads and skills move between environments. Commitments and single-region designs do nothing for portability.","Section 1.1",["A multi-year commitment ties the company more tightly to one provider, which increases rather than reduces lock-in.","Using one region affects availability and latency but does nothing to make workloads portable to another provider.","The Archive storage class is about cost for rarely read data; it does not make data or applications more portable.",null],null],["cdl13",0,1,"A retailer moved its servers to the cloud a year ago, but release cycles, approvals and team structures are unchanged and little has improved. What is most likely missing?",["A larger machine type for each server so the migrated applications run faster than before","A second cloud provider","More storage capacity","Cultural and process change alongside the technology"],3,"Digital transformation is about changing how people and processes work, not only where servers run. Without new ways of working (small empowered teams, automation, experimentation) the benefits stay small.","Section 1.1",["Bigger machines make servers faster but leave the slow approvals and team structures, the real problem, unchanged.","A second cloud provider adds complexity without changing how the retailer's people and processes work.","Storage capacity is not the constraint; release cycles and approvals are process and culture issues.",null],null],["cdl14",0,1,"Which scenario is the best example of SaaS?",["A company's staff use Google Workspace for email and documents","A developer deploys code to App Engine","An administrator creates Compute Engine VMs and installs a database","A team runs containers on a GKE cluster it configures"],0,"SaaS is a complete application consumed by users, such as Google Workspace. App Engine is PaaS, Compute Engine is IaaS, and GKE is a managed container platform where the team still configures workloads.","Section 1.3",[null,"App Engine is platform as a service: the developer still writes and deploys the application code.","Creating VMs and installing a database is infrastructure as a service, where the customer manages the OS and software.","Configuring and running containers on a GKE cluster is a managed platform, not a finished application consumed by users."],null],["cdl15",0,1,"A company must launch a pilot product in a new country within weeks and may cancel it after three months. Why is the cloud a good fit?",["Cloud resources can only be bought in three-year terms","It removes all compliance obligations in the new country because the provider handles them for customers","Resources can be provisioned quickly in nearby regions and released if the pilot ends","It guarantees the product will succeed"],2,"Agility and pay-as-you-go let teams experiment quickly without long-term hardware commitments, and global regions put the pilot close to users. Compliance obligations still apply in the new country.","Section 1.1",["Cloud resources are normally pay-as-you-go with no term; commitments are optional discounts, not a requirement.","Compliance obligations in the new country still apply; the provider helps with its share, but the customer remains responsible for its own.",null,"No platform guarantees business success; the cloud only makes it cheaper and faster to test the idea."],null],["cdl16",0,2,"A media company stores millions of photos, videos and PDF contracts with no fixed schema. What type of data is this?",["Structured data","Semi-structured data","Relational data","Unstructured data"],3,"Images, video and documents have no predefined data model, so they are unstructured data, usually kept in object storage such as Cloud Storage. JSON with tags and keys would be semi-structured.","Section 2.1",["Structured data fits fixed rows and columns, which photos, videos and PDFs do not.","Semi-structured data has tags or keys, like JSON; media files and scanned contracts have no such internal structure.","Relational data is structured data in related tables, the opposite of files with no fixed schema.",null],null],["cdl17",0,2,"A company must keep backup files for seven years to satisfy auditors and expects to read them less than once a year. Which Cloud Storage class is the most cost-effective?",["Standard","Nearline","Archive","Coldline"],2,"Archive has the lowest storage price and suits data read less than once a year, with a 365-day minimum storage duration. Coldline suits roughly quarterly access and Nearline monthly access, at higher storage prices.","Section 2.2",["Standard has the highest storage price and is meant for frequently accessed data, not backups read less than once a year.","Nearline suits data read about once a month and costs more to store than Archive for seven-year retention.",null,"Coldline suits roughly quarterly access; Archive is cheaper for data read less than once a year."],null],["cdl18",0,2,"A web team serves product images to customers all day, every day, from Cloud Storage. Which storage class fits?",["Standard","Archive","Coldline","Nearline"],0,"Standard is for frequently accessed (hot) data and has no minimum storage duration or retrieval fees. The colder classes lower the storage price but add retrieval costs that would be expensive for constant reads.","Section 2.2",[null,"Archive has a 365-day minimum and retrieval fees on every read, so constant image serving would be very expensive.","Coldline charges retrieval fees on every read and is meant for data read about once a quarter.","Nearline adds retrieval fees and is designed for data read about once a month, not all day."],null],["cdl19",0,2,"An existing application uses a PostgreSQL database that the company wants to stop patching and backing up by hand, with as few code changes as possible. What should it use?",["Bigtable","Cloud SQL for PostgreSQL","Firestore","BigQuery, loading the tables into a data warehouse"],1,"Cloud SQL is a managed relational database service for MySQL, PostgreSQL and SQL Server that handles patching, backups and replication. Bigtable and Firestore are NoSQL and would need a rewrite; BigQuery is an analytics warehouse.","Section 2.2",["Bigtable is a wide-column NoSQL database; moving a PostgreSQL app to it would require a rewrite.",null,"Firestore is a NoSQL document database, so the relational PostgreSQL schema and queries would have to be redesigned.","BigQuery is an analytics warehouse, not a transactional database for an application's day-to-day reads and writes."],null],["cdl20",0,2,"A global payments company needs a relational database with strong consistency that scales horizontally and serves users on several continents. Which service fits best?",["Cloud SQL","Firestore","Spanner","Cloud Storage"],2,"Spanner is a globally distributed relational database with strong consistency and horizontal scaling. Cloud SQL is regional and scales mainly vertically; Firestore is a document database.","Section 2.2",["Cloud SQL is a regional database that scales mainly vertically, so it does not fit global horizontal scale.","Firestore is a NoSQL document database, not a relational database with SQL transactions across continents.",null,"Cloud Storage is object storage for files, not a relational database with transactions."],null],["cdl21",0,2,"An IoT company ingests billions of sensor readings per day and needs very fast reads and writes of time-series data by device and timestamp. Which database fits best?",["Bigtable","Cloud SQL","Looker","AlloyDB"],0,"Bigtable is a wide-column NoSQL database built for huge volumes of low-latency, high-throughput data such as time series and IoT. Cloud SQL and AlloyDB are relational databases, and Looker is a BI tool.","Section 2.2",[null,"Cloud SQL is a relational database on a single primary instance and is not built for billions of writes per day.","Looker is a business intelligence tool for reports and dashboards; it does not store sensor data.","AlloyDB is a high-performance PostgreSQL database, but massive time-series ingestion by key is Bigtable's specialty."],null],["cdl22",0,2,"A mobile game needs a serverless database that syncs player data to devices in real time and still works when a phone goes offline. Which service fits best?",["Bigtable","BigQuery","Cloud SQL for MySQL","Firestore"],3,"Firestore is a serverless NoSQL document database with real-time listeners and offline support for mobile and web clients. BigQuery is for analytics and Bigtable targets massive analytical and operational throughput, not client sync.","Section 2.2",["Bigtable targets huge analytical and operational throughput and has no built-in real-time sync to mobile devices or offline mode.","BigQuery is an analytics warehouse, not an app database that syncs data to player devices.","Cloud SQL for MySQL is relational and server-based, with no built-in real-time client listeners or offline support.",null],null],["cdl23",0,2,"Analysts want to run SQL across several terabytes of sales history from many systems without managing any servers or indexes. What should they use?",["Cloud SQL","Memorystore","Firestore","BigQuery"],3,"BigQuery is a serverless data warehouse that scans large datasets with SQL and separates storage from compute. Cloud SQL is an operational database for applications and is not designed for this scale of analytics.","Section 2.2",["Cloud SQL runs application transactions and is not designed to scan terabytes of history from many systems.","Memorystore is an in-memory cache (Redis or Memcached), not a SQL data warehouse.","Firestore is a document database for apps and does not support large-scale SQL analytics.",null],null],["cdl24",0,2,"A logistics company wants delivery-truck GPS events analyzed within seconds and stored for dashboards. Which pair of services is the typical pipeline?",["Pub/Sub to ingest events and Dataflow to process them into BigQuery","Transfer Appliance to ingest and Looker Studio to process","Cloud Storage Archive to ingest the events and Cloud SQL to process and aggregate them every night","Cloud Run functions to ingest and Nearline to process"],0,"Pub/Sub ingests streaming events and delivers them asynchronously; Dataflow transforms them in real time and writes to BigQuery. Transfer Appliance is for bulk offline transfer, and storage classes do not process data.","Section 2.3",[null,"Transfer Appliance is a physical device for bulk offline transfer, and Looker Studio visualizes data rather than processing streams.","Archive storage is for rarely read data and a nightly Cloud SQL job cannot analyze events within seconds.","Nearline is a storage class that cannot process data, and it is meant for data read about once a month."],null],["cdl25",0,2,"A company has many existing Apache Spark and Hadoop jobs and wants to run them in Google Cloud with minimal changes. Which service fits?",["Dataflow","Pub/Sub","Dataproc","Looker"],2,"Dataproc is managed Spark and Hadoop, so existing jobs move with little change. Dataflow runs Apache Beam pipelines, which would mean rewriting the jobs.","Section 2.3",["Dataflow runs Apache Beam pipelines, so existing Spark and Hadoop jobs would need to be rewritten.","Pub/Sub is a messaging service that moves events; it cannot run Spark or Hadoop jobs.",null,"Looker is a BI tool for exploring data; it does not execute Spark or Hadoop processing."],null],["cdl26",0,2,"Different departments report different revenue numbers because each defines 'revenue' in its own spreadsheet. Which Google Cloud capability best addresses this?",["Looker with a governed semantic model so metrics are defined once","Moving every spreadsheet to Coldline storage","Creating a separate BigQuery dataset for each department so every team can keep its own definitions","Giving every analyst the Owner role"],0,"Looker's semantic layer (LookML) defines business metrics once so every report uses the same definition. Separate datasets or broader permissions would make the inconsistency worse.","Section 2.3",[null,"Moving spreadsheets to Coldline only changes where the files are stored, not how revenue is defined.","Separate datasets with separate definitions would lock in the inconsistency instead of fixing it.","The Owner role grants broad permissions and does nothing to make metric definitions consistent."],null],["cdl27",0,2,"A company must move 400 TB from its data center to Cloud Storage but has a slow internet connection that would take months. What is the best option?",["Storage Transfer Service over the existing connection","Database Migration Service","BigQuery Data Transfer Service","Transfer Appliance"],3,"Transfer Appliance is a physical device shipped to the customer, filled with data and shipped back to Google, which avoids months of network transfer. The other services all move data over the network.","Section 2.2",["Storage Transfer Service moves data over the network, which the scenario says would take months on the slow connection.","Database Migration Service migrates databases to Cloud SQL or AlloyDB, not 400 TB of files to Cloud Storage.","BigQuery Data Transfer Service schedules loads into BigQuery from SaaS and other sources, not bulk file transfer from a data center.",null],null],["cdl28",0,2,"A marketing team wants data from Google Ads and other SaaS sources loaded into BigQuery on a regular schedule without writing code. What should they use?",["Storage Transfer Service, copying exported files from each source into Cloud Storage","BigQuery Data Transfer Service","Transfer Appliance","Database Migration Service"],1,"BigQuery Data Transfer Service schedules managed loads from supported SaaS applications and other sources into BigQuery. Database Migration Service moves databases into Cloud SQL or AlloyDB.","Section 2.2",["Storage Transfer Service copies files into Cloud Storage; it does not load Google Ads data into BigQuery tables on a schedule.",null,"Transfer Appliance is a physical device for one-off bulk transfers, not scheduled loads from SaaS sources.","Database Migration Service moves operational databases into Cloud SQL or AlloyDB, not SaaS data into BigQuery."],null],["cdl29",0,2,"A healthcare company wants analysts to find trusted datasets, see who owns them and ensure only approved people can view sensitive columns. Which practice is this?",["Data governance","Data archiving","Streaming analytics","Load balancing"],0,"Data governance covers discovering and cataloging data, ownership, quality, and access policies such as restricting sensitive fields. Archiving and streaming are about storage and movement, not control.","Section 2.1",[null,"Archiving moves rarely used data to cheaper storage; it does not help people find trusted data or restrict sensitive columns.","Streaming analytics processes events in real time; it does not cover ownership, cataloging or access policy.","Load balancing spreads network traffic across servers and has nothing to do with managing data."],null],["cdl30",0,3,"Which statement best describes machine learning?",["A set of if-then rules written by programmers in advance to handle every possible case the system meets","A system that learns patterns from data to make predictions or decisions","Any software that stores data in the cloud","A type of database optimized for analytics"],1,"Machine learning systems learn patterns from example data instead of being explicitly programmed with rules. Hand-written rules are traditional programming, even when they are complex.","Section 3.1",["Hand-written if-then rules are traditional programming; ML learns patterns from data instead.",null,"Storing data in the cloud is not machine learning; ML is about learning from data to predict or decide.","A database optimized for analytics is a data warehouse such as BigQuery, not machine learning."],null],["cdl31",0,3,"A marketing team wants a tool that writes first drafts of product descriptions from a few bullet points. What kind of AI is this?",["Predictive analytics","Rule-based automation","Generative AI","Optical character recognition"],2,"Generative AI creates new content such as text, images or code. Predictive models forecast or classify from existing data, and OCR only reads text from images.","Section 3.1",["Predictive analytics forecasts or classifies from existing data; it does not write new product descriptions.","Rule-based automation follows fixed logic and cannot compose varied new text from a few bullet points.",null,"OCR reads existing text from images; it does not create new content."],null],["cdl32",0,3,"A company calculates sales tax with a fixed formula set by law. A manager suggests using ML for it. What is the best response?",["ML is needed because tax is a numerical problem","ML should be used so the formula can adjust itself automatically whenever tax laws change in the future","A pre-trained Vision API should be used","ML is a poor fit because the rules are known and exact, so normal code is better"],3,"ML shines when patterns are too complex to write as rules. When exact, stable rules exist, conventional code is cheaper, explainable and always correct; a model could only approximate them.","Section 3.1",["Being numerical does not make a problem suited to ML; a known formula is computed exactly by ordinary code.","Tax law changes are published rules that developers update in code; a model adjusting itself would only approximate the law.","The Vision API analyzes images and has nothing to do with calculating tax.",null],null],["cdl33",0,3,"A loan-approval model trained mostly on data from one city performs poorly for applicants from rural areas. What data quality problem does this show?",["The training data was not representative of the people the model serves","The model was stored in the wrong region","The data was encrypted at rest","There was too little compute available during training, so the model could not finish learning the patterns"],0,"When training data does not represent the full population, the model can be inaccurate or unfair for under-represented groups. Encryption and region choice do not affect what the model learned.","Section 3.1",[null,"The storage region of a model does not change what it learned or how accurate it is for rural applicants.","Encryption at rest protects data confidentiality but has no effect on what the model learns.","More compute speeds up training, but it cannot teach patterns that are missing from unrepresentative data."],null],["cdl34",0,3,"A company deploying an AI model for hiring decisions wants to be able to tell candidates why the model made a recommendation. Which responsible AI practice is this?",["Data residency","Data compression","Autoscaling","Explainability"],3,"Explainability means being able to describe which factors drove a prediction, which supports fairness reviews and accountability. It is one of the practices behind Google's AI Principles.","Section 3.1",["Data residency is about where data is stored, not about explaining a model's recommendation.","Data compression reduces storage size and has nothing to do with explaining predictions.","Autoscaling changes capacity with load and does not help explain why a model made a decision.",null],null],["cdl35",0,3,"A retailer wants to automatically read text from photos of printed receipts that customers upload, and has no ML team. What is the fastest option?",["Train a custom model from scratch with Vertex AI custom training on labeled receipts","Build a Spanner database","Call the pre-trained Cloud Vision API text detection","Use BigQuery ML linear regression"],2,"The pre-trained Vision API already detects text in images and needs no training data or ML expertise. Custom training would take far longer and a regression model does not read images.","Section 3.2",["Custom training from scratch needs ML expertise, lots of labeled data and time, while a pre-trained API already reads text.","Spanner is a relational database; it stores data but cannot read text from images.",null,"Linear regression predicts numbers from tabular data and cannot read text from a photo."],null],["cdl36",0,3,"A support center wants to translate customer chat messages between 20 languages in real time. Which Google Cloud option fits with the least effort?",["The Cloud Translation API","AutoML with a custom dataset","A Bigtable instance","Training a model on TPUs"],0,"The Translation API is a pre-trained service for translating text between many languages. Training your own translation model is only worth it for highly specialized vocabulary.","Section 3.2",[null,"AutoML needs a custom labeled dataset and training, which is unnecessary when a pre-trained translation service exists.","Bigtable is a NoSQL database and does not translate anything.","Training a translation model on TPUs is a large custom project, the most effort rather than the least."],null],["cdl37",0,3,"Data analysts who know SQL but not Python want to forecast next quarter's sales from data already in BigQuery. What is the best option?",["Export the data to spreadsheets and hire a data science team to build a model in Python","Use BigQuery ML to create and run the model with SQL","Use the Cloud Vision API","Move the data to Firestore first"],1,"BigQuery ML lets analysts create, train and use models with SQL where the data already lives, with no data movement. The Vision API is for images.","Section 3.2",["Exporting data and hiring a team adds time and cost, when the analysts can build the model in BigQuery with SQL.",null,"The Vision API analyzes images, not sales tables.","Firestore is a document database for apps and would move data away from where it can be analyzed and modeled."],null],["cdl38",0,3,"A manufacturer wants to detect defects specific to its own products in assembly-line images. It has thousands of labeled images but no ML experts. What is the best fit?",["The pre-trained Natural Language API","Custom training with hand-written TensorFlow code","A Cloud SQL database","AutoML image training in Vertex AI"],3,"AutoML trains a custom model on your own labeled data with little ML expertise. Pre-trained APIs do not know these specific defects, and hand-written custom training needs data scientists.","Section 3.2",["The Natural Language API analyzes text, not images of product defects.","Hand-written custom training needs ML experts, which the manufacturer does not have.","Cloud SQL could store results, but a database cannot learn to recognize defects in images.",null],null],["cdl39",0,3,"A company's data science team has a unique recommendation algorithm that is a key competitive advantage and needs full control over the model code. What should it use?",["A pre-trained API","Only BigQuery ML's built-in models","Looker Studio","Vertex AI custom training"],3,"Custom training on Vertex AI gives full control of the code, framework and model for differentiated problems, at the cost of more expertise and time. Pre-trained APIs cannot be customized to that degree.","Section 3.2",["A pre-trained API solves common tasks and cannot be controlled at the code level for a unique, differentiating algorithm.","BigQuery ML's built-in model types are convenient but do not give full control over custom model code.","Looker Studio builds dashboards and reports; it does not train models.",null],null],["cdl40",0,3,"A team wants to compare several foundation models, including Google's and open models, before choosing one to build on in Vertex AI. Where should it look?",["Vertex AI Model Garden","Cloud Storage Nearline","The Cloud Billing console","Security Command Center"],0,"Model Garden is the catalog in Vertex AI for discovering, testing and deploying Google, partner and open models. The other options are storage, billing and security tools.","Section 3.2",[null,"Nearline is a Cloud Storage class for infrequently accessed data, not a catalog of models.","The billing console shows costs; it does not let you discover or compare foundation models.","Security Command Center reports security risks and threats, not AI models."],null],["cdl41",0,3,"A company's internal chatbot built on a large language model sometimes gives confident but made-up answers about HR policies. What approach best reduces this?",["Increase the number of GPUs serving the model so that it has more capacity to think through each answer","Store the chatbot logs in Archive storage","Ground the model's answers in the company's approved HR documents","Remove all users' access to the chatbot"],2,"Grounding connects the model to trusted, current sources such as company documents, so answers are based on real content and hallucinations are reduced. More hardware does not make answers more accurate.","Section 3.2",["More GPUs make responses faster or allow more users but do not make the model's facts correct.","Changing where logs are stored does nothing to the accuracy of the chatbot's answers.",null,"Removing all access avoids wrong answers only by abandoning the tool, rather than fixing its accuracy."],null],["cdl42",0,3,"What are Tensor Processing Units (TPUs)?",["Google-designed chips that accelerate machine learning workloads","A type of Cloud Storage bucket for training data","A pricing discount for AI services","Google's managed Kubernetes nodes that run web applications and scale them automatically on demand"],0,"TPUs are custom accelerators (ASICs) that Google designed for machine learning training and inference. They are offered in Google Cloud alongside GPUs.","Section 3.2",[null,"TPUs are processors, not storage buckets; training data would live in Cloud Storage or BigQuery.","TPUs are hardware accelerators, not a pricing or discount program.","GKE nodes are VMs for running containers; TPUs are specialized ML chips that can be attached to some workloads."],null],["cdl43",0,3,"A business leader asks which factor matters most when choosing between a pre-trained API, AutoML and custom training. Which answer is best?",["The color scheme of the console","The number of IAM roles in the project","Which region has the most zones, since AI services must run in the largest region available to be effective","How unique the problem is, and the time, data and ML skills available"],3,"The trade-off is effort versus differentiation: pre-trained APIs are fastest for common tasks, AutoML suits custom data with little expertise, and custom training suits unique problems when skilled people and time are available.","Section 3.2",["The console's appearance has no bearing on which AI approach suits a business problem.","The number of IAM roles is a security matter, not a factor in choosing between pre-trained, AutoML and custom models.","AI services do not need the region with the most zones; region choice is about latency, residency and availability.",null],null],["cdl44",0,4,"A company must leave its data center in three months when the lease ends and has no time to change its applications. Which migration approach fits?",["Refactor the applications into microservices running on GKE before the lease ends","Reimagine the business process","Retire every application","Rehost (lift and shift) onto Compute Engine VMs"],3,"Rehosting moves applications as they are, which is the fastest way to exit a data center on a deadline. Refactoring and reimagining deliver more cloud value but take far longer.","Section 4.1",["Refactoring into microservices takes far longer than three months and the company has no time to change applications.","Reimagining the business process is the most time-consuming approach and does not fit a hard deadline.","Retiring every application would shut down systems the business still needs.",null],null],["cdl45",0,4,"During a migration assessment, a team finds a reporting tool that nobody has used for two years. What is the best migration decision for it?",["Rehost it","Refactor it","Retire it","Replatform it"],2,"Applications that are no longer used should be retired rather than migrated, which reduces cost and effort. Moving an unused tool in any form wastes money.","Section 4.1",["Rehosting an unused tool spends migration effort and ongoing cloud cost on something nobody uses.","Refactoring takes the most effort of all, which makes no sense for an unused application.",null,"Replatforming still moves and runs the tool in the cloud, which wastes money when nobody uses it."],null],["cdl46",0,4,"A team moves a web application to Compute Engine but switches its self-managed MySQL database to Cloud SQL, without rewriting the application. Which approach is this?",["Rehost","Retain","Refactor into microservices","Replatform"],3,"Replatforming (move and improve) makes targeted changes, such as adopting a managed database, to gain some cloud benefits without re-architecting. A pure rehost would keep the self-managed database.","Section 4.1",["A pure rehost would keep the self-managed MySQL database; adopting Cloud SQL is a targeted improvement.","Retain means leaving the application where it is, but this one is moving to Compute Engine.","Refactoring changes the code and architecture, while this application's code is not being rewritten.",null],null],["cdl47",0,4,"A web tier on Compute Engine must add VMs automatically when CPU load rises and replace any VM that fails its health check. What should the team use?",["A single large VM with a bigger disk","A managed instance group with autoscaling and autohealing","A Cloud Storage bucket","A committed use discount on the existing VMs so extra capacity costs less during peaks"],1,"Managed instance groups create identical VMs from a template, autoscale on signals such as CPU, and recreate unhealthy instances. A single VM can neither scale out nor heal itself, and discounts change price, not behavior.","Section 4.2",["A single VM cannot add instances or replace itself when it fails, however large its disk.",null,"A Cloud Storage bucket stores objects; it does not run or scale a web tier.","A committed use discount lowers the price of VMs but does not create or heal instances automatically."],null],["cdl48",0,4,"A company runs hundreds of VMware virtual machines on-premises and wants to move them to Google Cloud quickly while keeping its VMware tools and processes. Which service fits?",["Google Cloud VMware Engine","Cloud Run with each VM repackaged as a container","App Engine","Firestore"],0,"Google Cloud VMware Engine runs a native VMware environment in Google Cloud, so existing VMs, tools and skills carry over. Cloud Run and App Engine would require repackaging the applications.","Section 4.2",[null,"Repackaging hundreds of VMs as containers is a large rework, not a quick move that keeps VMware tools.","App Engine runs application code on a managed platform and cannot host existing VMware virtual machines.","Firestore is a document database and cannot run virtual machines."],null],["cdl49",0,4,"A company runs a specialized database that must stay on dedicated physical servers for licensing reasons, but it wants low-latency access from Google Cloud services. Which option fits?",["Cloud Run functions","Spot VMs","Bare Metal Solution","Cloud Storage Coldline"],2,"Bare Metal Solution provides dedicated physical servers close to Google Cloud regions for specialized workloads such as some Oracle databases. Serverless and Spot VMs are shared, virtualized options.","Section 4.2",["Cloud Run functions runs short event-driven code on shared serverless infrastructure, not a database on dedicated physical servers.","Spot VMs are shared, interruptible virtual machines, the opposite of dedicated physical servers for a critical database.",null,"Coldline is a storage class for rarely read data and cannot run a database."],null],["cdl50",0,4,"What is the main reason containers make applications portable?",["They package the application with its dependencies so it runs the same in any environment","They include a full guest operating system for every application, so nothing depends on the host machine at all","They can only run on one cloud provider","They remove the need for any code changes when scaling a database"],0,"A container bundles the code, runtime and libraries it needs and shares the host's kernel, so it runs consistently on a laptop, on-premises or in any cloud. Containers are lighter than VMs because they do not carry a full guest OS.","Section 4.2",[null,"It is VMs that carry a full guest OS; containers share the host kernel, which is why they are lighter.","Containers run on any platform with a container runtime; portability across providers is their main benefit.","Containers package applications; they do not remove code changes needed to scale a database."],null],["cdl51",0,4,"A company wants to run Kubernetes but does not want to manage, size or patch worker nodes, and wants to pay for the resources its pods request. Which option fits?",["GKE Standard mode","GKE Autopilot mode","Self-managed Kubernetes on Compute Engine","Bare Metal Solution"],1,"In Autopilot, Google manages the nodes and you pay for the resources pods request. In Standard mode the customer manages and pays for the node pools, and self-managed Kubernetes adds even more work.","Section 4.2",["In Standard mode the customer manages the node pools and pays for the node VMs, which the company wants to avoid.",null,"Self-managed Kubernetes means running the control plane and nodes yourself, the most operational work of all.","Bare Metal Solution provides dedicated physical servers and is not a managed Kubernetes service."],null],["cdl52",0,4,"A developer has a stateless containerized API that gets no traffic at night and bursts during the day. The team wants no infrastructure to manage and no cost when idle. Which service fits best?",["Compute Engine with a fixed number of VMs","GKE Standard with three always-on nodes","Cloud Run","Bare Metal Solution"],2,"Cloud Run runs containers serverlessly, scales automatically with requests and can scale to zero so idle time costs nothing. Fixed VMs and always-on nodes keep charging overnight.","Section 4.3",["A fixed number of VMs keeps running and billing overnight and needs infrastructure management.","Always-on GKE nodes charge even with no traffic and still need cluster management.",null,"Bare Metal Solution is dedicated physical hardware for specialized workloads, with ongoing cost and management."],null],["cdl53",0,4,"A small piece of code must run each time a file is uploaded to a Cloud Storage bucket, to create a thumbnail. Which option is the simplest fit?",["A managed instance group","An event-driven function with Cloud Run functions","A GKE cluster","Google Cloud VMware Engine"],1,"Cloud Run functions (formerly Cloud Functions) runs short single-purpose code in response to events such as a new object in a bucket, with no servers to manage. The other options need far more setup for one small task.","Section 4.3",["A managed instance group keeps VMs running and needs setup, far more than a small thumbnail task requires.",null,"A GKE cluster is a full container platform, heavy for one small event-triggered function.","VMware Engine runs whole VMware environments and is not meant for small event-driven code."],null],["cdl54",0,4,"A company has a large monolithic application where a small change requires redeploying everything and one bug can bring down the whole system. What modernization is usually proposed?",["Moving it to a larger single VM","Breaking it into microservices that are deployed and scaled independently","Storing its logs in Archive storage","Removing its load balancer"],1,"Microservices split an application into small services with their own deployments, so teams release independently and failures are isolated. A bigger VM keeps the same coupling.","Section 4.1",["A larger VM keeps the same single deployment unit, so small changes still redeploy everything and one bug still affects all of it.",null,"Where logs are stored does not change how the application is deployed or how failures spread.","Removing the load balancer reduces resilience and does nothing to decouple the application."],null],["cdl55",0,4,"A bank wants to let fintech partners use its account-balance service securely, with rate limits, API keys, usage analytics and a developer portal. Which Google Cloud product fits?",["Cloud Armor","Pub/Sub","Apigee","Cloud CDN"],2,"Apigee is Google Cloud's API management platform: security, quotas and rate limiting, analytics, versioning and developer portals. Cloud Armor protects against web attacks but does not manage APIs as products.","Section 4.4",["Cloud Armor protects web apps from DDoS and attacks, but it does not provide API keys, developer portals or usage analytics as products.","Pub/Sub moves messages between systems; it does not manage partner access, rate limits or portals for APIs.",null,"Cloud CDN caches content at the edge and has no API management features."],null],["cdl56",0,4,"A company runs Kubernetes clusters in its own data centers, in Google Cloud and in another cloud, and wants to manage configuration and security policy for all of them consistently. Which option fits?",["GKE Enterprise","Cloud SQL","App Engine","Compute Engine sole-tenant nodes"],0,"GKE Enterprise (formerly Anthos) manages fleets of Kubernetes clusters across Google Cloud, on-premises and other clouds with consistent configuration and policy. The other options run in Google Cloud only.","Section 4.5",[null,"Cloud SQL is a managed relational database and does not manage Kubernetes clusters.","App Engine is a platform for web apps inside Google Cloud and does not manage clusters in other environments.","Sole-tenant nodes give dedicated hosts in Google Cloud only and do not manage clusters elsewhere."],null],["cdl57",0,4,"A company runs a nightly batch job that can be interrupted and restarted without harm and wants the lowest possible VM price. Which Compute Engine option fits?",["Sole-tenant nodes","Spot VMs","Custom machine types with extra memory","Always-on standard VMs with no discounts"],1,"Spot VMs offer large discounts on spare capacity but can be stopped at any time, which suits fault-tolerant batch work. Sole-tenant nodes cost more because they reserve physical hosts.","Section 4.2",["Sole-tenant nodes reserve whole physical hosts and cost more, not less.",null,"Custom machine types match size to need but are priced like standard VMs, without the large spare-capacity discount.","Standard VMs with no discounts are the most expensive option for an interruptible job."],null],["cdl58",0,4,"An application needs a specific older operating system and full control over installed software and kernel settings. Which compute option fits best?",["Cloud Run","App Engine standard environment","Cloud Run functions","Compute Engine virtual machines"],3,"Compute Engine VMs give full control over the OS and software, which legacy or specialized workloads need. Serverless platforms manage the OS for you and restrict what you can change.","Section 4.2",["Cloud Run manages the operating system for you, so you cannot choose an older OS or change kernel settings.","App Engine standard provides managed runtimes and does not allow control over the OS or kernel.","Cloud Run functions runs code on a managed runtime with no access to the underlying operating system.",null],null],["cdl59",0,5,"An attacker changes the amounts in stored payment records without anyone noticing. Which security property was violated?",["Confidentiality","Availability","Integrity","Privacy"],2,"Integrity means data is accurate and has not been altered without authorization. Confidentiality would be violated if the records were read by someone unauthorized, and availability if they could not be reached.","Section 5.1",["Confidentiality is broken when data is seen by unauthorized people; here the data was changed.","Availability is about being able to reach systems and data; the records were still available, just altered.",null,"Privacy concerns how personal data is collected and used; the core failure here is unauthorized modification."],null],["cdl60",0,5,"A storage bucket holding customer files was made publicly readable by mistake. Under the shared responsibility model, whose responsibility was this setting?",["Google's, because Google runs the storage service and is therefore responsible for all of its settings","The customer's, because it controls configuration and access to its data","The internet service provider's","No one's, because public access cannot be restricted"],1,"Customers are responsible for configuring access to their data; Google secures the underlying service. Misconfiguration by customers is one of the most common causes of cloud data exposure.","Section 5.1",["Google secures the storage service itself, but access settings on the customer's bucket are the customer's responsibility.",null,"The internet provider carries traffic and has no control over bucket permissions.","Public access can be restricted with IAM and organization policies such as public access prevention."],null],["cdl61",0,5,"A finance employee receives an email that looks like it is from the CEO, asking them to sign in through a link to approve an urgent payment. What threat is this and what control helps most?",["Phishing; phishing-resistant two-step verification such as security keys","Ransomware; a larger VM","A DDoS attack; the Archive storage class","Misconfiguration; a committed use discount"],0,"This is phishing that tries to steal credentials. Security keys and other phishing-resistant 2SV stop stolen passwords alone from granting access, and user awareness training helps people spot the lure.","Section 5.1",[null,"The email is a credential-stealing lure, not ransomware, and a larger VM does nothing against it.","A DDoS attack floods a service with traffic; a storage class is not a defense against anything here.","This is a social-engineering attack, not a configuration mistake, and a pricing discount is not a security control."],null],["cdl62",0,5,"Which approach best describes zero trust?",["Trust every device on the corporate network","Block all remote workers","Verify every access request using identity, device and context, regardless of network location","Rely on one strong perimeter firewall"],2,"Zero trust assumes no implicit trust from network location. Each request is checked with identity, device state and context and granted least privilege. Google's BeyondCorp is its implementation of this model.","Section 5.1",["Trusting every device on the network is the old perimeter model that zero trust replaces.","Zero trust enables secure remote work by verifying each request; it does not block remote workers.",null,"A single perimeter firewall is the castle-and-moat model; zero trust removes trust based on network location."],null],["cdl63",0,5,"A security team uses IAM, firewall rules, encryption, logging and a web application firewall together, so that if one control fails others still protect the data. What is this approach called?",["Single sign-on","Defense in depth","Data residency","Lift and shift"],1,"Defense in depth layers several independent controls so one failure does not expose the system. Single sign-on is only one identity control.","Section 5.1",["Single sign-on is one identity control, not a strategy of layering many independent controls.",null,"Data residency is about where data is stored, not layering security controls.","Lift and shift is a migration approach, not a security principle."],null],["cdl64",0,5,"How is customer data stored in Google Cloud protected at rest by default?",["It is not encrypted unless the customer turns on encryption for each bucket, disk and database","It is encrypted only for Archive storage","It is encrypted by default with Google-managed keys, with no action needed","It is encrypted only if the customer uploads its own keys"],2,"Google Cloud encrypts all customer data at rest by default using Google-managed keys. Customers can choose customer-managed keys in Cloud KMS for more control, but encryption does not depend on it.","Section 5.2",["Encryption at rest is on by default in Google Cloud; customers do not need to turn it on per resource.","All customer data is encrypted at rest by default, whatever the storage class.",null,"Customer-supplied or managed keys are optional; data is encrypted by default even without them."],null],["cdl65",0,5,"A regulator requires a company to control when its encryption keys are rotated and to be able to disable them itself. What should it use?",["Customer-managed encryption keys (CMEK) in Cloud KMS","Default Google-managed encryption only","Coldline storage","A larger number of IAM basic roles"],0,"CMEK lets the customer create, rotate, disable and destroy the keys that protect its data in supported services. Default encryption is automatic but managed entirely by Google.","Section 5.2",[null,"Default Google-managed encryption protects data, but Google controls rotation, so the company cannot disable the keys itself.","Coldline is a storage class for rarely read data and gives no control over encryption keys.","Basic IAM roles are broad permissions and have nothing to do with key rotation; they also weaken least privilege."],null],["cdl66",0,5,"A contractor needs to view Compute Engine instances in one project but must not change anything. What is the best practice?",["Grant the Owner role on the organization","Grant the Editor role on the project so the contractor does not need to ask for more access later","Share an administrator's password","Grant a view-only Compute role on that project only"],3,"Least privilege means the narrowest role on the smallest scope that still lets the person do the job. Owner or Editor would allow changes, and sharing passwords removes accountability.","Section 5.2",["Owner on the organization gives full control of everything, far beyond viewing instances in one project.","Editor allows changing resources, which the contractor must not do, and granting extra access in advance breaks least privilege.","Sharing an administrator's password gives full admin rights and removes accountability for who did what.",null],null],["cdl67",0,5,"Which Google Cloud IAM statement is correct?",["Roles are granted to principals, and each role is a collection of permissions","Permissions are granted directly to users one at a time","Basic roles such as Editor are recommended for production least privilege","IAM applies only to Compute Engine"],0,"In IAM you grant roles, which bundle permissions, to principals such as users, groups and service accounts on a resource. Basic roles are broad and are discouraged for production.","Section 5.2",[null,"In IAM you grant roles, which bundle permissions; permissions are not granted to users one by one.","Basic roles are broad and discouraged in production; predefined or custom roles support least privilege.","IAM controls access across almost all Google Cloud services, not just Compute Engine."],null],["cdl68",0,5,"A public website is hit by a large distributed denial-of-service attack and by attempts at SQL injection. Which Google Cloud service helps defend against both at the edge?",["Cloud KMS","Cloud Armor","VPC Service Controls","Assured Workloads"],1,"Cloud Armor provides DDoS protection and web application firewall rules for applications behind Google Cloud's external load balancers. Cloud KMS manages keys and VPC Service Controls limits data exfiltration from services.","Section 5.2",["Cloud KMS manages encryption keys and does not filter web attacks or absorb DDoS traffic.",null,"VPC Service Controls limits data exfiltration from managed services; it does not protect public websites from DDoS.","Assured Workloads applies compliance controls such as data location; it does not block web attacks."],null],["cdl69",0,5,"A company wants to reduce the risk that data in BigQuery and Cloud Storage is copied out to projects outside the company, even by someone with valid credentials. What should it use?",["Cloud CDN","Spot VMs","VPC Service Controls","Looker Studio"],2,"VPC Service Controls creates service perimeters around Google Cloud services to reduce data exfiltration, even when credentials are stolen. The other options are not security controls.","Section 5.2",["Cloud CDN caches content at the edge and does not stop data being copied out of BigQuery.","Spot VMs are discounted, interruptible VMs, not a data protection control.",null,"Looker Studio builds dashboards and can itself be used to view data; it does not prevent exfiltration."],null],["cdl70",0,5,"A security team wants one place in Google Cloud to see misconfigurations, vulnerabilities and threats across all its projects. What should it use?",["Security Command Center","Cloud Billing reports","Database Migration Service","Vertex AI Studio"],0,"Security Command Center gives a centralized view of security posture, vulnerabilities, misconfigurations and threats across an organization. Billing reports show costs, not risks.","Section 5.2",[null,"Billing reports show spending, not misconfigurations or threats.","Database Migration Service moves databases and has no security posture features.","Vertex AI Studio is for prototyping with generative AI models, not security monitoring."],null],["cdl71",0,5,"An auditor asks who deleted a Compute Engine instance last Tuesday. Where would an administrator look first?",["Cloud Storage lifecycle rules","The Carbon Footprint report","Cloud Audit Logs (Admin Activity) in Cloud Logging","The Apigee developer portal"],2,"Cloud Audit Logs record administrative actions such as creating and deleting resources, including who did it and when. Admin Activity logs are always on.","Section 5.2",["Lifecycle rules change or delete Cloud Storage objects by age; they do not record who deleted a VM.","The Carbon Footprint report estimates emissions, not administrative actions.",null,"The Apigee developer portal documents APIs for developers and holds no record of Compute Engine deletions."],null],["cdl72",0,5,"A European government agency must keep data in the EU and restrict support access to EU personnel to meet a regulatory framework. Which Google Cloud offering is designed for this?",["Cloud CDN","Assured Workloads","Spot VMs","Firestore"],1,"Assured Workloads applies controls for regulated workloads, such as data location and personnel access restrictions, to help meet compliance requirements. A CDN deliberately copies content to many locations.","Section 5.3",["Cloud CDN copies content to edge locations worldwide, the opposite of keeping data in the EU.",null,"Spot VMs are discounted interruptible VMs and provide no residency or personnel controls.","Firestore is a database; choosing its location helps residency but it does not restrict support personnel."],null],["cdl73",0,5,"A customer wants to see logs showing when Google staff accessed its content and the reason for the access. Which feature provides this?",["Access Transparency","Cloud Armor","Cloud Monitoring uptime checks","Committed use discounts"],0,"Access Transparency provides logs of actions Google personnel take on customer content, with a justification. Access Approval goes further by requiring customer approval first.","Section 5.3",[null,"Cloud Armor protects web applications from attacks and does not log Google staff access.","Uptime checks test whether an endpoint responds; they do not record personnel access.","Committed use discounts are a pricing option, not a transparency feature."],null],["cdl74",0,5,"A prospective customer's compliance team asks for Google Cloud's ISO 27001 certificate and SOC 2 report. Where can it get them?",["From the Carbon Footprint page","By opening a Security Command Center finding","From Google's Compliance Reports Manager","Only by visiting a Google data center"],2,"Google publishes third-party audit reports and certificates, such as ISO/IEC 27001 and SOC reports, through its Compliance Reports Manager. Security Command Center shows the customer's own security findings.","Section 5.3",["The Carbon Footprint page reports emissions, not security certifications or audit reports.","Security Command Center findings describe risks in the customer's own environment, not Google's audit reports.",null,"Reports are downloaded online; data center visits are not how customers get certificates."],null],["cdl75",0,6,"Engineers launch resources freely, but finance only sees the total bill a month later and cannot tell which team caused a cost spike. Which practice best addresses this?",["Giving every engineer the Owner role","Moving all workloads to one region","Turning off all monitoring to save money","FinOps: shared cost visibility and accountability between finance, engineering and business teams"],3,"FinOps brings finance, technology and business teams together with shared, timely cost data and accountability. Broad roles and less monitoring would make the problem worse.","Section 6.1",["Giving everyone Owner adds security risk and does nothing to show which team caused which cost.","Consolidating regions does not give finance visibility into which team is spending.","Turning off monitoring removes visibility, the opposite of what finance needs.",null],null],["cdl76",0,6,"In the Google Cloud resource hierarchy, what does a folder usually represent?",["A single virtual machine and its attached persistent disks and network interfaces","A department or team that groups projects","A billing invoice","A storage class"],1,"Folders sit between the organization and projects and group projects, often by department, team or environment. Folders can contain other folders.","Section 6.1",["A VM and its disks are resources at the bottom of the hierarchy, not folders.",null,"Invoices come from Cloud Billing accounts; folders organize projects.","Storage classes are Cloud Storage settings and are not part of the resource hierarchy."],null],["cdl77",0,6,"Which order correctly lists the Google Cloud resource hierarchy from top to bottom?",["Project, folder, organization, resource","Organization, project, folder, resource","Resource, project, folder, organization","Organization, folder, project, resource"],3,"The organization is the root, folders group projects, projects contain resources such as VMs and buckets. Projects are also the basis for enabling APIs and linking billing.","Section 6.1",["This list is upside down; the organization is the root, not the project.","Folders sit between the organization and projects, not below projects.","This is the hierarchy reversed; resources are at the bottom, not the top.",null],null],["cdl78",0,6,"An administrator grants a group the Viewer role on a folder. What access does the group get to projects inside that folder?",["None, because roles never pass down","Viewer access to every project in the folder, through inheritance","Owner access to every project","Viewer access only to projects created after the grant"],1,"Allow policies are inherited by everything below the node where they are set, including existing and new projects. That is why broad grants high in the hierarchy should be made carefully.","Section 6.1",["Roles granted on a folder are inherited by everything below it.",null,"Inheritance passes on the role that was granted, Viewer, not a more powerful one.","Inheritance covers existing projects in the folder as well as ones created later."],null],["cdl79",0,6,"The company must ensure no one in any project can create resources outside the EU, even project owners. What is the best control?",["An organization policy restricting resource locations, set at the organization node","A label named region=eu on each project","A budget alert","An email to every team asking them to use EU regions, followed by a quarterly manual review"],0,"Organization policies set guardrails that are inherited down the hierarchy and apply regardless of IAM role, such as allowed resource locations. Labels and budgets do not block anything.","Section 6.1",[null,"A label is just a key-value tag for organizing and billing; it does not block resource creation anywhere.","A budget alert warns about spending and cannot prevent resources being created outside the EU.","An email and a later review rely on people complying and detect problems after the fact instead of preventing them."],null],["cdl80",0,6,"A team lead wants an email when a project's monthly spend reaches 80% of its planned amount. What should be configured?",["A quota on the number of VMs","An organization policy","A Cloud Billing budget with an alert threshold","A committed use discount"],2,"Budgets track actual or forecast spend and send alerts at thresholds you choose. Note that a budget alerts but does not stop spending on its own.","Section 6.1",["A quota limits resource usage such as vCPUs; it does not send spend notifications at 80% of a planned amount.","Organization policies restrict configurations such as locations; they do not track spending.",null,"A committed use discount lowers prices for steady usage but does not send alerts."],null],["cdl81",0,6,"A company wants to break down its Google Cloud costs by team and environment and analyze them with SQL. What should it do?",["Apply labels to resources and export billing data to BigQuery","Create one billing account per virtual machine so each machine's costs appear on a separate invoice","Move all resources into the Archive storage class","Disable billing reports and estimate by hand"],0,"Labels are key-value pairs that appear in billing data, and billing export to BigQuery allows detailed SQL analysis and dashboards by label. One billing account per VM is unmanageable.","Section 6.1",[null,"A billing account per VM is unmanageable and still would not group costs by team and environment.","Storage classes apply only to Cloud Storage objects and have nothing to do with cost reporting.","Disabling reports removes the data needed for analysis; estimating by hand is inaccurate."],null],["cdl82",0,6,"A company has a steady database workload that will run 24x7 for at least three years. Which pricing option usually saves the most?",["Committed use discounts for a one- or three-year term","Spot VMs","On-demand pricing with no commitment","Using a larger machine type than needed"],0,"Committed use discounts trade a one- or three-year commitment for a lower price on predictable, steady usage. Spot VMs can be stopped at any time, which does not suit a database that must stay up.","Section 6.1",[null,"Spot VMs can be stopped at any time, which does not suit a database that must run 24x7.","On-demand pricing has no commitment but misses the savings available for steady, long-term usage.","Oversizing machines increases cost rather than saving money."],null],["cdl83",0,6,"Which statement about Site Reliability Engineering (SRE) is correct?",["SRE forbids any automation of operations work","SRE is a billing model for cloud services","SRE means operations teams never talk to developers, and each group keeps its own separate goals","SRE applies software engineering to operations, for example automating toil and using SLOs"],3,"SRE, which started at Google, treats operations as a software problem: automate repetitive toil, set SLOs, use error budgets and run blameless postmortems. It is a way to implement DevOps ideas.","Section 6.2",["SRE actively automates repetitive operational work (toil); it does not forbid automation.","SRE is a way of running reliable systems, not a pricing or billing model.","SRE brings developers and operations together with shared goals such as SLOs and error budgets.",null],null],["cdl84",0,6,"A service has an SLO of 99.9% successful requests over 30 days. Which term describes the 0.1% of requests that are allowed to fail?",["The service level agreement","The service level indicator","The recovery point objective","The error budget"],3,"The error budget is the allowed unreliability, 100% minus the SLO. The SLI is the measured success rate, and the SLA is the external contract with consequences.","Section 6.2",["An SLA is the external contract with consequences, not the allowed 0.1% of failures.","The SLI is the measurement itself, such as the success rate, not the allowance for failure.","The RPO is about acceptable data loss in disaster recovery, not an SLO allowance.",null],null],["cdl85",0,6,"A cloud provider promises customers service credits if monthly uptime falls below a stated level. What is this promise?",["A service level indicator","A service level agreement","An error budget","A recovery time objective"],1,"An SLA is a contract with customers that states consequences, such as credits, when a level is missed. SLOs are internal targets and SLIs are the measurements.","Section 6.2",["An SLI is a measurement of service behavior; it carries no promise of credits.",null,"An error budget is the internal allowance of unreliability, not a customer contract.","A recovery time objective is a disaster recovery target for restoring service, not a contractual promise."],null],["cdl86",0,6,"After an outage, a business says it can lose at most 15 minutes of order data and must be running again within 2 hours. Which statement is correct?",["RPO is 2 hours and RTO is 15 minutes","Both values describe the SLA credit","RPO is 15 minutes and RTO is 2 hours","Both values describe the error budget"],2,"The recovery point objective is the maximum acceptable data loss measured in time, and the recovery time objective is how quickly service must be restored. Mixing them up is a common exam trap.","Section 6.2",["This swaps the two: data loss is measured by the RPO, and time to restore by the RTO.","SLA credits are contractual compensation, not recovery objectives for data loss and downtime.",null,"An error budget is the unreliability allowed by an SLO, not a disaster recovery target."],null],["cdl87",0,6,"An operations team wants dashboards of CPU and request latency and an alert when error rates rise, for services across several projects. Which Google Cloud service fits?",["Cloud Armor","Looker Studio","Cloud Monitoring","Cloud KMS"],2,"Cloud Monitoring collects metrics, builds dashboards, runs uptime checks and sends alerts. Cloud Logging stores and searches logs; Cloud Armor and Cloud KMS are security services.","Section 6.2",["Cloud Armor is a web application firewall and DDoS protection service, not a metrics and alerting tool.","Looker Studio builds business dashboards from data sources but is not designed for infrastructure metrics and alerting.",null,"Cloud KMS manages encryption keys and has no monitoring features."],null],["cdl88",0,6,"A developer wants to find which step of a request across several microservices is making the whole request slow. Which tool is designed for this?",["Cloud Trace","Cloud Billing reports","Compliance Reports Manager","Transfer Appliance"],0,"Cloud Trace collects latency data as requests travel between services, showing where time is spent. Logs and metrics help too, but tracing is built to follow one request end to end.","Section 6.2",[null,"Billing reports show costs, not the latency of steps within a request.","Compliance Reports Manager provides audit reports and certificates, not performance data.","Transfer Appliance is a device for moving large data sets offline."],null],["cdl89",0,6,"A company running business-critical workloads wants the fastest response to critical issues and a named technical contact who knows its environment. What should it consider?",["Relying only on community forums","A higher-tier Google Cloud Customer Care plan such as Premium","Changing all VMs to Spot VMs","Moving to a single zone"],1,"Paid Customer Care plans offer faster response times, and the top tier adds proactive services such as a Technical Account Manager. Community forums have no response commitments.","Section 6.2",["Community forums have no response commitments and no named contact for critical production issues.",null,"Spot VMs can be stopped at any time, which reduces reliability instead of improving support.","Moving to a single zone reduces resilience and does not change the level of support."],null],["cdl90",0,6,"A company wants to reduce the carbon emissions of a new workload that has no strict location requirements, and to report its cloud emissions. What should it do?",["Run the workload in every region at once","Choose a region marked as low CO2 and use the Carbon Footprint tool","Keep the workload on-premises","Use the Archive storage class for all data"],1,"Google marks regions with low grid carbon intensity in the console, and the Carbon Footprint tool reports the emissions associated with a customer's usage. Running everywhere would increase emissions.","Section 6.3",["Running in every region multiplies resource use and emissions rather than reducing them.",null,"Staying on-premises gives up Google's efficient data centers and carbon-free energy work, and the Carbon Footprint tool only reports cloud usage.","Archive storage lowers storage cost for rarely read data but is not a strategy for reducing a workload's emissions."],null]]);
